all right let's jump into audience  questions for the week love getting  questions from people in the audience  these are more curated than live calls  as some of you may have noticed then  people sometimes like both sometimes  like these sometimes prefer the live  calls because they're more spontaneous  let's go to the first one and then we  can evaluate first question today David  now that the midterms are over what's  next for the left should we be working  within the Democratic Party up until  2020 or should we be working outside of  the 20 of the Democratic Party who  should run in 2020 for the Democrats  okay so let's start with the  inside-outside thing  the debate over inside or outside the  Democratic Party has been actually  growing a lot in the last seven to ten  days and what people often mean by work  within or outside of the Democratic  Party is do we try as progressives to  move the Democratic Party to the left  with the idea that the Democratic Party  still is the path forward for  progressivism in the United States or do  we abandon the Democratic Party and try  to foment progressivism in the United  States by working within third parties  I've talked to you about this before I  don't like the two-party duopoly in the  United States that being said it's gonna  be very tough at the federate the  national level for real third party  success until number one you have some  kind of serious campaign finance reform  and number two I would argue you have  some kind of rank choice voting put in  place I know that some people standard  rank choice voting they don't like  because you can end up with somebody  who's no one's first choice winning  there are other tweaked versions that  solve that problem so I think that for  me path the path forward is at the local  level absolutely particularly in  nonpartisan elections or elections where  it doesn't matter you don't necessarily  designate or run on a party platform  let's get people from third parties  involved let's start moving in the  direction of it not mattering whether  you're a Democrat if you're progressive  that you can  when that's gonna start at the local  level I think the unfortunate reality  and this is where I know people will get  mad at me pat and say I am a blind  pragmatist the pragmatic element of my  personality tells me that at the federal  level right now there are more  opportunities to move the Democrats to  the left pushing for congressional  candidates to not accept PAC money as  ROH Khanna and some others have chosen  to do I think there are more practical  opportunities there than saying we're  abandoning the Democratic Party and  we're going third party I just think  that it's just not going to work both  our uphill battles that's for sure but I  think that the path of least resistance  is a progressive takeover of the  Democratic Party I think that's that's  certainly easier to do because many  people are not willing to educate  themselves on some of these third  parties they're gonna enter the voting  booth and either check their the the box  that says D or check the box that says R  and that's just how most people operate  and I also think that if there was ever  a chance for a third party to do well it  would have been the 2016 election  because you had Donald Trump versus  Hillary Clinton two of the most if not  the most unpopular candidates of all  time and we didn't see that we didn't  see Gary Johnson or Jill Stein really  get any traction so I I think that the  easiest thing to do and it's not easy  but easiest is to try to take over the  Democratic Party and push it to the left  the the other sort of element that I  think is important about this is that if  you have progressive ideas coming from  third parties it makes it much easier  for the Democratic Party to ignore those  and to keep the Overton Window centered  straddling the the political spectrum  exactly as it does right now once you  inject more progressive ideas to the  Democratic Party's discussion I believe  that they have less of a simple means by  which they can ignore the fact that  these progressive ideas are getting more  attention case in point if there was no  discussion of medicare-for-all within  the Democratic Party if you only saw  medicare-for-all discussion relegated to  third parties and the furthest  that you got in the Democratic Party on  healthcare was a public option for  example you would have a Democratic  Party that was even more to the right of  where it is right now on this issue so I  think that there is this you I've talked  about the term unilateral radicalization  unilateral unilateral movement to the  right by extremists on the rights I  don't think that we need Stalinist  Leninists on the left in order to  progressive eyes the discussion on the  left you just need serious social  democracy on the left the ideas of  Bernie Sanders when it comes to college  and health insurance and so on and so  forth and that is going to immediately  put the Democratic Party in a position  to explain why they are ignoring these  voices if they are and if you relegate  those ideas to third parties it's much  easier for Democrats to ignore them so I  think local level third party's federal  level do everything you can to  progressive eyes the Democratic Party  such that maybe one day we can do real  campaign finance reform and and deal  with the problems of the voting system  that we have send me your thoughts I  know for sure that there are a lot of  people in the audience who just disagree  with me

